{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6d9","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a91f-5a05-7370-bf74-ffa98261cad4"},"sequence":455,"recorded_at":1780531400000905,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d48d36b9-6599-476a-89c2-ba7d2fcc06eb","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"d48d36b9-6599-476a-89c2-ba7d2fcc06eb","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d48d36b9-6599-476a-89c2-ba7d2fcc06eb"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0a920-c2d2-7752-9f2f-dd2a1217b0f9"},"task_id":"01a0a920-c2d2-7752-9f2f-dd2a1217b0f9","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0a920-c2d2-7752-9f2f-dd2a1217b0f9","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Documents/Codex/2026-09-15/new-chat-4/work/skills/skill-swift-relux/SKILL.md`.\n1|---\n2|name: swift-relux\n3|description: >-\n4|  Implement and review Swift features using the relux-works Relux stack:\n5|  swift-relux, SwiftUIRelux, ReluxRouter, state/reducer modules, Saga/Flow,\n6|  runtime composition, temporal state, and Relux analytics integration.\n7|  Use for Relux-based apps (Swift Relux, свифт-релакс, релакс модуль,\n8|  релакс роутер), not generic SwiftUI, analytics, or localization tasks.\n9|---\n10|\n11|# Swift Relux\n12|\n13|Use this skill for Swift codebases that use the `relux-works` Relux stack:\n14|\n15|- `swift-relux` / product `Relux`\n16|- `swiftui-relux` / product `SwiftUIRelux`\n17|- `swiftui-reluxrouter` / product `ReluxRouter`\n18|\n19|## Resource Routing\n20|\n21|Load only the files needed for the task.\n22|\n23|Instruction flows:\n24|\n25|- App composition, IoC, root runtime bootstrap:\n26|  read [references/instructions/app-bootstrap.md](references/instructions/app-bootstrap.md).\n27|- Feature module authoring, reducers, flows, tests, analytics:\n28|  read [references/instructions/module-authoring.md](references/instructions/module-authoring.md).\n29|- SwiftUI containers, views, temporal state, pull-to-refresh:\n30|  read [references/instructions/ui-integration.md](references/instructions/ui-integration.md).\n31|- Logout, session reset, store cleanup:\n32|  read [references/instructions/logout-cleanup.md](references/instructions/logout-cleanup.md).\n33|\n34|Reference sections:\n35|\n36|- Ecosystem package selection, version boundaries, dispatch semantics:\n37|  [references/overview/ecosystem.md](references/overview/ecosystem.md).\n38|\n39|- Core state management, actions, effects, reducers, sagas, flows:\n40|  [references/core/relux-core.md](references/core/relux-core.md).\n41|- SwiftUI app/root integration, containers, local UI state, temporal state:\n42|  [references/swiftui/swiftui-relux.md](references/swiftui/swiftui-relux.md).\n43|- Navigation stacks, `Router`, `ProjectingRouter`, route actions:\n44|  [references/router/relux-router.md](references/router/relux-router.md).\n45|- Feature module layout, namespace naming, reducers, tests:\n46|  [references/modules/module-conventions.md](references/modules/module-conventions.md).\n47|- Swift package boundaries, public namespace facades, resources, tests:\n48|  [references/packages/package-conventions.md](references/packages/package-conventions.md).\n49|- Skill scope, target libraries, current non-goals:\n50|  [references/overview/micro-spec.md](references/overview/micro-spec.md).\n51|\n52|Reusable snippets:\n53|\n54|- [references/snippets/dispatch-runtime-selection.md](references/snippets/dispatch-runtime-selection.md)\n55|- [references/snippets/ioc-registry.md](references/snippets/ioc-registry.md)\n56|- [references/snippets/modular-projecting-router.md](references/snippets/modular-projecting-router.md)\n57|- [references/snippets/store-cleanup.md](references/snippets/store-cleanup.md)\n58|- [references/snippets/swiftui-container-page.md](references/snippets/swiftui-container-page.md)\n59|- [references/snippets/temporal-state.md](references/snippets/temporal-state.md)\n60|- [references/snippets/refreshable-perform-async.md](references/snippets/refreshable-perform-async.md)\n61|- [references/snippets/product-analytics.md](references/snippets/product-analytics.md)\n62|- [references/snippets/localization.md](references/snippets/localization.md)\n63|\n64|## Default Workflow\n65|\n66|- Inspect the nearest `Package.swift`, app composition root, and existing module\n67|  layout before proposing Relux changes.\n68|- Check the project's resolved package revisions before copying APIs; see the\n69|  ecosystem reference for source evidence and compatibility boundaries.\n70|- Prefer existing package and namespace patterns over creating a new local style.\n71|- Keep state changes in reducers. Do not mutate Relux state directly from flows.\n72|- Model side effects as `Effect` handled by `Flow` or `Saga`.\n73|- Choose dispatch APIs by runtime identity, not reachability. Use top-level\n74|  `action` / `actions` for the initialized primary runtime, use unqualified\n75|  instance helpers inside `Saga` / `Flow`, and target an explicit\n76|  `Relux` / `Dispatcher` only when a host-library lifecycle integration\n77|  supplies the exact runtime or an isolated integration test owns its\n78|  dispatcher/logger event bus.\n79|- Attach SwiftUI temporal state with `.reluxTemporal(state:)`. The modifier\n80|  resolves Relux from the SwiftUI environment; do not pass or use a runtime\n81|  instance at the view call site merely to connect temporal state.\n82|- Do not inject view-owned temporal state into a `Flow`. It is not currently a\n83|  stable runtime-resolvable dependency; carry immutable `Sendable` input until\n84|  a future runtime state accessor defines lookup and lifetime semantics.\n85|- Prefer actor isolation for flows, sagas, and stateful asynchronous\n86|  dependencies such as services, fetchers, repositories, caches, and storage\n87|  adapters. Keep immutable/stateless implementations as value types when they\n88|  own no serialized mutable state.\n89|- Use `@MainActor` for UI/platform ownership and for adapters around synchronous\n90|  third-party APIs that require main-thread access, including specific WebRTC\n91|  or Unity bridges when their contracts demand it. Keep isolation at that\n92|  boundary instead of spreading it through unrelated business code.\n93|- Make cross-task protocols and values `Sendable`; treat\n94|  `@unchecked Sendable`, locks, and shared mutable classes as reviewed\n95|  exceptions rather than defaults.\n96|- Use `HybridState` for simple SwiftUI-observed features; split into\n97|  `BusinessState` plus `UIState` when business data is shared, transformed, or\n98|  aggregated across domains.\n99|- Treat skeleton code as scaffolding only. Do not add fake product state, fake\n100|  SDK contracts, placeholder effects, or test-only behavior that pretends to be\n101|  real domain logic.\n102|- When changing behavior, add focused Swift Testing coverage for reducers,\n103|  flow handling, module registration, or SwiftUI integration as appropriate.","final_result":true}}}
